VIRGIN PLANET
Poul Anderson (1959) — depicted on the 2000 Baen omnibus cover with Star Ways
Pulp science fiction → 813.54  
American fiction • 20th‑century • science fiction & pulp adventure

TYPE
Hard science fiction / lost-colony adventure / planetary romance
Psychotechnic League future history

VARIABLES
protagonist     : Davis Bertram (young explorer, owns his own starship)
captor          : Corporal Barbara Whitley (red-haired cavalrywoman on giant flightless bird)
ally            : Valeria
antagonists     : The Doctors (priest-scientists who monopolize cloning)
setting         : Atlantis (Earth-like moon, no native mammals)
reproduction    : parthenogenesis machine (cloning, 300 years running)
society         : all-female, pre-industrial towns, men exist only as myth (“Fathers”)

FUNDAMENTALS
- Colony ship of women wrecked 300 years earlier, isolated by space anomaly
- Women forgot what men look like; only large animals are giant birds
- Davis lands, immediately lassoed and treated as possible “Monster”
- Rival towns fight over possession of the first man and his ship
- Doctors want him dead to keep their power
- Davis + Barbara + Valeria break the monopoly
- Planet reopened to the rest of humanity

STORY
Davis Bertram becomes the first man to reach the isolated moon Atlantis. The moment he steps off his ship a bird-riding woman lassos him. The women have waited centuries for the legendary Men to return, yet they do not recognize one when he arrives. Some want to claim him, others want to kill the “monster.” Factions go to war. Davis spends the book being dragged between towns, proving he is human, staying alive, and discovering how an entire society has reproduced without men. He allies with Barbara and Valeria, ends the Doctors’ control of the cloning technology, and brings Atlantis back into contact with the wider human universe.
